  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 226 PM EDT Fri Sep 4 2026\n\nModels continue to point towards a transition to a drier and \nsomewhat cooler pattern, but as has been the case the past several \ndays confidence in the finer details of this remain uncertain. \n\nThe quasi-stationary boundary remains in place from southern WI to \nnorthern parts of our forecast area and into Ohio with a series of \noutflow boundaries from multiple clusters of storms south of the \nboundary. A line of showers and storms moved off the lake and\nwas progressing SE, but has underwent a weakening trend over the\npast hour or so. Additional storms are trying to get going along\na outflow boundary in portions of Cass Co to Jay county IN.\nExpect widely scattered to scattered storms to persist ahead of\nthese area with a window of little/no convection. Somewhat \nbetter upper level dynamics and another wave arrive this evening\nwith limited consensus on isol-sct storms re-developing, but \ntiming, strength and location differences persist. Will keep any\npops no higher than chance across mainly NE half of the area \nthis evening with a slow southward trend overnight (and \ndecreasing pops). Slight risk for severe continues, but \nconfidence in overall threat is also low as best shear likely to\nremain near the front. For Saturday, wherever the front finally\nsettles in late AM into the afternoon will likely see at least \nsome isolated to maybe scattered development. Model blend is \nrather messy and although tempted to make some changes, just not\nenough confidence mess with anything. \n\nSunday through Tuesday still appear to be cooler and less humid with \nmore pleasant highs in the upper 70s to low 80s and lows in the 50s \nas the upper level ridge retrogrades slightly to the west. This \ndoesn't last long with a push back northeast occuring for the second \nhalf of the week and subsequent return to chances for convection \nand/or increasing heat and humidity.",
